          "question": "Which alarm features in reflux does guidance say should change the plan?",
          "answer": "**Vomiting blood or passing black, tarry stools needs emergency assessment today. Don't wait for an appointment.** The gastrointestinal bleeding guidance says any presentation that matches it is escalated for emergency assessment. While waiting, keep the person lying down and keep them warm, and bring a list of their medicines, especially blood thinners and anti-inflammatory drugs. If there is bleeding with dizziness or fainting, seek emergency care now. (Sources: Gastrointestinal bleeding guidance; GERD alarm-features guideline.)\n\nThe GERD guideline, \"GERD: empiric initial management and the alarm features that change it,\" lists features that mean the usual trial of acid-reducing medicine should be skipped in favour of **prompt endoscopy** (a camera test of the upper gut):\n\n- Difficulty swallowing, or pain on swallowing\n- Unintentional weight loss\n- Gastrointestinal bleeding\n- Iron-deficiency anaemia\n- Persistent vomiting\n- A palpable mass, or a family history of upper gastrointestinal cancer\n- New symptoms starting at an older age\n\nThe sources do not say how fast \"prompt\" is in practice, so ask a clinician for a timeframe. The guideline also gives no numerical age cut-off for \"older age.\" Persistent vomiting that stops someone keeping fluids down, or feeling faint, confused or very unwell, needs care straight away. That point comes from general safety guidance, not from the reflux sources.\n\nThe retrieved material reports no disagreement between authorities on this list, so I cannot say that authorities differ. It also does not grade the evidence behind each feature, so I cannot say how strongly each one is supported. If any of these features apply to you, discuss them with a clinician, who can decide whether endoscopy is needed. If you describe your own symptoms, I can assess their urgency.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
